WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.150
Safe first products: ingredients, hygiene and dilution.

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.890
This is the video for module 1 of Organic Skincare and Hair Products.

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:23.220
This course starts with products that contain no water. Makers call them anhydrous, which means water-free. Germs need water to grow, so oil-only products do not need a preservative against germs.

4
00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:30.820
Their main enemy is rancidity. Oils react with air, light and heat, and start to smell like old groundnuts.

5
00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:40.820
A lotion or cream mixes oil and water. The moment water is present, germs can grow, often without any smell you can notice.

6
00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:46.990
So the rule is simple. If it has water in it, it needs a real preservative and proper training.

7
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:54.620
You will make four products. Whipped shea butter, for dry skin and for sealing moisture into natural hair.

8
00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:58.790
Body oil, applied after a bath on damp skin.

9
00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:04.520
Hair and scalp oil, for oiling the scalp, sealing ends and adding shine.

10
00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:10.490
And a simple balm with beeswax, for lips, dry patches and cracked heels.

11
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.960
Never make bleaching creams, or anything with hydroquinone, mercury or steroids.

12
00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:27.660
Every product is built from three families. Butters give body. Shea butter is soft and rich, and melts close to skin temperature. Cocoa butter is much harder.

13
00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:39.450
Liquid oils give spread and softness. A light oil sinks in quickly. A heavy oil like castor is thick and sticky, so use it as a smaller part of a blend.

14
00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:48.830
Wax gives structure. Beeswax melts at roughly 62 to 64 degrees Celsius, so balms stay firm in the heat.

15
00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:54.000
Oils soften and protect hair, but do not promise that any oil makes hair grow.

16
00:01:55.250 --> 00:02:02.590
Clean work separates a professional from a hobbyist. Clear the table of food, pets and children, and tie back your hair.

17
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.470
Wash the table, then wipe it with 70% alcohol and let it dry.

18
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:15.800
Wash bowls, spatulas and jars with hot soapy water, rinse well, and dry them completely.

19
00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:25.920
Wipe tools and the inside of jars with 70% alcohol, and let them air-dry fully. The alcohol must evaporate before you fill.

20
00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:34.830
Wear clean gloves, and do not touch your face or phone while working. Keep these tools for products only, never for cooking.

21
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.280
A single drop of water left in a jar gives germs a place to start.

22
00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:51.370
Essential oils are very concentrated plant extracts. Used too strong, they can burn or sensitise skin. Always dilute them in a carrier oil.

23
00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.520
For sensitive or damaged skin, 0.2 to 1%.

24
00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:01.440
For face products, 0.5 to 1.2%.

25
00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:05.640
For body oils and butters, 1 to 3%.

26
00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:10.340
For scalp oils, this course stays at 1% or below.

27
00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:19.420
For babies and young children, leave essential oils out completely. Advise pregnant customers to check with their health worker.

28
00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:31.170
Funke in Ibadan is making 250 g of whipped shea butter for adult body use. She chooses 2%, inside the 1 to 3% body range.

29
00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:42.830
250 g times 2, divided by 100, is 5 g of lavender. The other 245 g is her butter and oil blend.

30
00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:52.930
She places the bowl on the scale, presses tare to zero, and adds lavender drop by drop until the scale shows 5.00 g.

31
00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.000
She records it on her batch sheet.

32
00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:00.680
Drop sizes vary by oil and bottle, so always weigh.

33
00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:14.900
Some oils need extra care. Tea tree, peppermint, cinnamon and clove are known skin irritants. Keep them low, and keep peppermint and eucalyptus away from the faces of babies and small children.

34
00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:25.690
Cold-pressed citrus oils, like lemon, lime and bergamot, can cause burns on skin in the sun. Choose steam-distilled versions or other oils.

35
00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:36.880
And never let anyone swallow essential oils. A small child drinking a bottle is a medical emergency. Go to a hospital immediately, with the bottle.

36
00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:49.030
To remember. Start with water-free products. Anything with water needs a real preservative and training. Clean, sanitise with alcohol, and let everything dry.

37
00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:54.560
Weigh your essential oils and stay within the ranges. And leave them out of baby products.

38
00:04:55.810 --> 00:05:04.620
Your turn. Ask five people which natural products they buy, and choose up to three water-free products. Test three oils on your forearm.

39
00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:09.110
Then calculate three dilutions and photograph your clean workspace.

40
00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:11.400
Continue with the lessons below.
